7.2 Separating layout from content
Both documents contain the same words. Neither contains any of them:


paper.tex

%% Layout wrapper: the journal-ready version.
%% All of the actual writing is in main.tex, which this file and submission.tex
%% share.  Changing formats never means editing prose.
\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\input{preamble.tex}

\begin{document}
\maketitle
\input{main.tex}
\end{document}



submission.tex

%% Layout wrapper: the double-spaced, larger-type version some journals require
%% for review.  Same content, different layout.
\documentclass[12pt]{article}
\input{preamble.tex}

\begin{document}
\doublespacing
\maketitle
\input{main.tex}
\end{document}

The prose is in main.tex, which both wrappers \input. This costs you nothing on the first draft and saves you a great deal on the fourth, when the paper is going to a second journal with a different class file, and again when you post it to arXiv. You change the wrapper. You never touch a sentence.
Real projects extend this in the obvious way — a wrapper per journal, one for arXiv, one for the version with the supplement bound in — each a rule producing its own PDF, all sharing main.tex. The man-avg-cov project this example is modeled on has one for arXiv and one for Biometrics, the latter of which also splits the compiled PDF into a main document and a supplement with qpdf, because that journal requires them as separate uploads.
